<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-reasoning-taxonomy" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Reasoning Taxonomy</title>


  <introduction>
    <p>
      Understanding these reasoning patterns allows us to diagnose our own cognitive
      processes when building Eigenotes. We employ deductive reasoning to derive
      consequences from axioms, abductive reasoning to generate hypotheses from
      anomalous data, and spatial reasoning to visualize geometric structures.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <dl>
    <li>
      <title>Deductive Reasoning</title>
      <idx>deductive reasoning</idx>
      <p>Apply known principles to derive a specific conclusion.</p>
      <p><em>Example:</em> <q>Deriving the Boltzmann distribution from the maximum-entropy principle.</q></p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Inductive Reasoning</title>
      <idx>inductive reasoning</idx>
      <p>Generalizing from repeated observations.</p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          Given the analogous role of the partition function across several physical systems, what general principle
          might we infer about its role in statistical descriptions?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Temporal Reasoning</title>
      <idx>temporal reasoning</idx>
      <p>Reasoning involving the ordering, evolution, or dependence of events and states over time.</p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          Given an oscillator's initial phase and angular velocity, how does its state evolve over time according to its
          equation of motion?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Spatial Reasoning</title>
      <idx>spatial reasoning</idx>
      <p>Reasoning about spatial relationships, geometry, orientation, position, or symmetry.</p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          Given a two-dimensional potential-energy surface, where are the stable equilibrium points and how are they
          related according to their symmetries?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Causal Reasoning</title>
      <idx>causal reasoning</idx>
      <p>
        Determining how a change in one variable, condition, or mechanism produces or influences a change in another.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          How does a change in the temperature of a canonical ensemble affect corresponding changes in the Boltzmann
          distribution?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Comparative Analysis</title>
      <idx>comparative analysis</idx>
      <p>
        Evaluating similarities, differences, or relative behavior among multiple alternatives, systems, or conditions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          How does the principal-component structure of a system differ between two thermodynamic regimes?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Abstract Reasoning</title>
      <idx>abstract reasoning</idx>
      <p>
        Integrating concepts, relationships, or structures independent of a particular concrete instance or
        observation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          In what sense can the partition function be treated as a underlying generating structure of thermodynamic
          quantities across various physical systems?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Pattern Recognition</title>
      <idx>pattern recognition</idx>
      <p>
        Detecting and identifying recurring structures, consistencies, or relationships within observations, data, or
        sequences.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          A PCA of several thermodynamic states shows that the first principal component changes systematically with
          temperature. What underlying relation between temperature and the first principal component become evident
          in the projected data?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Statistical Reasoning</title>
      <idx>statistical reasoning</idx>
      <p>
        Drawing conclusions from data using probability theory, distributions, and sampling methods.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          Given noisy observations of an observable, what parameter value is most strongly supported by the data, and
          how uncertain is that estimate?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Abductive Reasoning</title>
      <idx>abductive reasoning</idx>
      <p>
        Inferring the most plausible explanation for an observation obtained from a data set with incomplete
        information about the system from which it was derived.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          An EEG signal changes drastically under a particular stimulus condition. What biophysical mechanism best
          accounts for this observation?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Hypothetical Reasoning</title>
      <idx>hypothetical reasoning</idx>
      <p>Predicting the result of assumed, hypothetical, or counterfactual conditions.</p>
      <p>
        <em>Example:</em>
        <q>
          Would a system of coupled oscillators synchronize according to the Kuramoto model if the internal frequencies
          followed a particular distribution?
        </q>
      </p>
    </li>
  </dl>
</section>